<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- PAI single writer (R-04). Per-transaction account-init verdict, immutable once
         written (a rerun must not flip CREATED to EXISTS). The unique key IS the full
         business identity, (arrival_id, sequence), so a restarted slice no-ops on its
         own committed rows via INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING rather than
         duplicating them.

         THIS IS THE RELATION prw READS. prw's DueSql.DUE_GATES counts rows in
         pai_verdict against dcre_pay, and this changeset is the only thing in the
         estate that creates it. That is why PAI's datasource default pointing at
         dcre_col was fatal rather than untidy: the creator built the table in the
         collections database while the reader looked for it in the payments one.

         SCRUM-107 converted this from a raw-SQL body to typed tags. Not cosmetic: a
         changelog extractor cannot see a relation minted inside a raw-SQL body, so an
         audit of "which service creates pai_verdict" answered NOBODY, which is
         precisely the reader-with-no-creator shape this project spent the day removing
         from ais_verdict. Typed tags make the creator visible to the same tooling that
         found the defect. The changeset keeps its id, so a database that has already
         run it would report a checksum change; none can, because the only database
         that ever ran it is dcre_col, which PAI no longer connects to, and dcre_pay
         has no pai_databasechangelog at all.

         UNGUARDED, deliberately, matching the v1 baselines elsewhere in the estate: an
         onFail="MARK_RAN" tableExists guard on a table only PAI creates could never
         fire, and a guard that cannot fire is not free. It would silently SKIP the
         create on the day the assumption stopped holding, turning a loud startup
         failure into a table that is quietly missing. -->
    <changeSet id="001-pai-verdict-pai" author="dcre">
        <createTable tableName="pai_verdict">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="arrival_id" type="UUID">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="sequence" type="INT">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <!-- EXISTS or CREATED -->
            <column name="action" type="VARCHAR(16)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="version" type="BIGINT" defaultValueNumeric="0">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="pai_verdict" columnNames="arrival_id, sequence"
                             constraintName="uq_pai_verdict_arrival_sequence"/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
